#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract==0.3.0",
#     "psycopg[binary]>=3.2",
#     "callva-harness-runner==0.8.0",
#     "pyyaml>=6",
# ]
# ///
"""tasks — what has to be done, and what was done about it.

A task is one piece of work with a goal. Its actions are the append-only record
of what was actually done to move it. The two together answer the question a
chat log cannot: what is open, what may be picked up now, and what already
happened.

It is a job queue: the agent's own queue of technical work, there so an agent
can work autonomously by taking the work set in front of it. It is not a
replacement for ClickUp, Asana or Notion, and ranking, a status vocabulary per
project, and holding a large backlog so nothing is lost are outside it. Order
comes from the worker's type order, `pickup_at` and `blocked_by`; tags filter;
anything else a consumer wants to carry goes in metadata, which takes any key
and any value and which `search` matches over.

READING
  tasks list [filters]            a scan: identity, state, and how much activity
                                  each task has. Soonest pickup first, or in the
                                  order `--sort` names
  tasks ready [filters]           the queue a worker consumes: every field and
                                  the whole trail, for what may be picked up now
  tasks show <task>               one task, every field, whole activity trail
  tasks meta show <task>          the whole metadata object
  tasks search <text> [filters]   the same scan, narrowed by substring over
                                  title, objective, description, unique key,
                                  assignee and the metadata JSON. Earliest
                                  created first, or in the order `--sort` names
  tasks counts [filters]          how many tasks hold each status, every status
                                  named and zero included, plus the total, for
                                  the filters `list` takes. Answers no rows
  tasks runs <task>               every raise of it: what held it, how long,
                                  and what came of each
  tasks runs --run SYSTEM:REF [--run ...] [--since WHEN]
             [--project ID | --all-projects]
                                  the raises those runs were, with the task
                                  each ran for, in one call. See RUNS
  tasks history <task> [--field F]   how its status, pickup, type and assignee
                                  moved, and what caused each move
  tasks watch [--project ID | --all-projects]
                                  stay connected and print one JSON line per
                                  change to a task that scope reads, as it
                                  happens, until stopped. See WATCH

WRITING  (needs allow_write on the connection; exit 4 otherwise)
  tasks add --type T --title TEXT [fields]      create one
  tasks set <task> [fields] [--clear FIELD]     edit scalar fields; with no
                                                field, a plain save that only
                                                moves updated_at
  tasks tag <task> <tag> [tag...]               add tags, never replacing
  tasks untag <task> <tag> [tag...]             remove tags by name
  tasks meta set <task> <key> <value> [<key> <value>...]
                                                set the keys named, all or none,
                                                leaving the rest as they were
  tasks meta rm <task> <key> [key...]           remove keys by name
  tasks activity <task> "what happened"          record real work done

EXECUTION  (also needs allow_write)
  tasks claim [filters | --key KEY] [--lease 3h] [--worker NAME] [--handler CMD]
                                  take one task that may be picked up now and
                                  hold it. Answers with the task in full, its
                                  trail, and the raise that now holds it, or
                                  with nothing when nothing is claimable.
                                  `--key` takes exactly that task and refuses,
                                  saying why, when it cannot be taken
  tasks release <execution> --outcome ok|failed|handback [--detail TEXT]
                     [--status S] [--assignee A] [--run SYSTEM:REF]
                     [--metrics JSON]
                                  close that claim and say what came of it
  tasks run <worker> [--key KEY] [--apply]
                                  one turn of the conveyor: claim by what that
                                  worker's `takes` selects, boot one harness turn on
                                  what it took, and settle the raise from where
                                  the turn left the task. Reports what it would
                                  take and writes nothing without `--apply`,
                                  and then needs no allow_write

  A claim opens a raise and writes nothing to the task: its status, assignee
  and pickup stay where they rest, and it is shown `in_progress` - by `show`,
  `list`, `search`, `ready`, `counts` and `watch`, and found by `--status
  in_progress` - for as long as an open raise holds it, a raise still running
  whose lease has not run out. `in_progress` is never stored, and no verb
  writes it. Once the raise ends the task shows the status it rests in, which
  is where the claim found it unless the work moved it.

  A claim is held by a lease and nothing else. Give one longer than the work
  itself can take: when the moment passes, the next claim closes the raise as
  abandoned and the task is free, still where it rested, and the first worker
  finds its claim gone at release. Recovering abandoned work has no schedule of
  its own - it happens inside `claim`, which is the only moment it matters.

  `release` names the raise rather than the task, and that is why the lapse is
  loud: a release addressed by task would close whichever claim it found, which
  after a lapse is somebody else\'s.

  The store refuses a second open raise on one task, so two workers cannot hold
  it even if two dispatchers try.

  A task an open raise still holds is not claimable, whatever its status says:
  it is held until that raise is released or its lease lapses, also when the
  turn moved it to a status a claim takes. A claim passes such a task over and
  takes the next one, and names it
  under `passed_over` - the task, `verdict` `held`, the raise as `execution`,
  and `why` with until when its lease runs - and so do `run` and the service's
  log line. `--key` naming one is refused, exit 6 `conflict`, naming the raise.

  The outcome scores the raise and moves nothing: closed `failed` or
  `handback`, the task stays exactly where it stands - where it rested, unless
  the work moved it. `--status` and `--assignee` move it, judged as `set`
  judges a move, and a move into `waiting` names whom it waits for or is
  refused with exit 6. `--outcome ok` alone, the one landing kept for a claim
  worked by hand, completes the task; with `--status` it lands there instead.

  `--run` records where the work actually ran, as a system and its handle - a
  pointer to the run's own record, wherever that is kept. A release without
  `--run` keeps the one the claim recorded, if any. `--metrics` takes a
  JSON object of whatever the runner measured: a duration, a token count, a
  price. The store keeps it and reads none of it, so a runner declares its own
  vocabulary instead of every consumer inheriting columns for one runner's. Bookkeeping stays out of `show` and out of the trail, so what a worker
  reads to act on a task is only what was learned about it.

ONE TURN OF THE CONVEYOR
  `run` is claim, boot and release in one call, and it is the only verb that
  starts a harness. It takes the next task the named worker's `takes` selects,
  boots one headless turn on it, and closes the raise against wherever that turn
  left the task. It never reads the turn's answer: the worker moves the status
  and writes the trail itself, through these same verbs, and `run` measures.

  `--key` takes exactly that task instead of the next one, and refuses out loud
  when it cannot be taken, the way `claim --key` does. `--apply` gates every
  write: without it nothing is claimed, no turn is started, and the answer says
  which task a claim would have taken.

  The turn is bounded twice and neither bound is the prompt's. The profile
  decides what it may run and its timeout kills the whole process tree; the
  raise decides what it may write, because `TASKS_EXECUTION` goes down with the
  turn and worker scope answers for every write it makes.

  The claim moved nothing, so where the turn left the task, against where it
  rested when it was claimed, is what scores the raise, and a turn that
  reports success and moved nothing is not believed. `run` closes the raise
  and never moves the task:

    complete, closed            ok - the work was done, or decided against
    moved to waiting or draft,  handback - it stopped at a gate, and the task is
    or waiting on another name  over to whoever has to answer
    a pickup it set still       ok, marked `waiting` - the turn was told to wait
    ahead                       on somebody and did, and the mark keeps a daily
                                check from spending an attempt
    todo, `stage` changed, or   ok, marked `handoff` - the turn ended at a
    assigned to someone else    boundary it named, or handed the task on to
                                whoever is assigned next; the next turn
                                continues from there
    moved from waiting to todo  ok - released, which is what it was taken for
    where it rested             failed, marked `cut_off` - the turn never
                                reached an ending of its own, and the task is
                                still where it rested, its assignee and `stage`
                                unchanged

  A turn the harness's account could not work - its usage limit spent, or not
  logged in - and one that fails in seconds having written nothing, are not
  attempts at the work: the raise is marked `exhausted`, and the count that
  parks a task does not spend it. The first holds the lane, as HOOKS states
  for the shipped after-raise handlers; neither writes anything to the task.
  At the ceiling the task is handed back to a person instead of dispatched
  again; in a project that sets an escalation chain, attempts count raises in
  place instead and the claim escalates the task rather than take it, as
  ESCALATION states. A task parked because its worker names a routine or a
  profile that is not there is marked `missing` and does not spend it either.

  A raise that left its task where it was - failed, or closed as abandoned -
  cools the task: no claim takes it again until the worker's
  `limits.cool_down_seconds` has passed since that raise ended. It is read from
  the raise, so it holds for a run by hand as for the service, and nothing is
  written to the task; a task taken from `waiting` stays waiting while it
  cools. A raise that moved its task cools nothing, and a task named with
  --key is taken whatever cools it.

  Every raise leaves exactly one entry. A turn that wrote nothing gets one
  saying so, because a task picked up that left no trace reads exactly like one
  never picked up, and telling those apart is how a stalled queue is noticed.

  On a claude profile the session a turn runs in is chosen before the claim,
  and the claim records it on the raise as `run_system` claude and `run_ref`
  the session id, so a raise that is cut off still names its transcript. A
  codex turn learns its id from the harness, and its raise names it when it is
  closed.

  A raise is cut off when it ended without an ending of its own: closed as
  abandoned, because its lease lapsed or `service stop --end-turns` ended its
  turn, or marked `cut_off`. When a worker on a claude profile claims a task whose last
  raise by that same worker was cut off and names a claude session, the turn
  resumes that session with a short message instead of the whole frame: which
  task, that the previous turn was cut off, to continue from where it stopped,
  and that the frame it started with still holds. The raise is new as always -
  its own `TASKS_EXECUTION`, its writes bound at this claim - names the resumed
  session, and is marked `resumed_from` with the raise it continues. A raise by
  another worker is never resumed. When that session's transcript is no longer
  on this machine, or the resume fails at launch - in seconds, writing
  nothing, and not for the account's limit - the turn starts a fresh session
  with the whole frame instead, and the raise's detail says so. A resumed raise
  counts against `attempts` like any other.

  A turn proves it is alive. Its claim holds the task by a short lease, and
  while the turn lives one writer renews that lease every beat - a sixth of
  the store retry window STORE describes, at least 2s and at most 30s apart -
  to the window and three beats more from the store's now: every 30s, to 270s,
  unless the window is changed. A renewal never reaches past
  `limits.lease_seconds` from the claim, which stays the longest one raise may
  hold its task. Each raise has exactly one lease writer:

    a turn the service started  the service, on its beat and again just before
                                it starts any turn, whose claim sweeps lapsed
                                leases, for as long as the turn's process is
                                still the one it recorded and never for a
                                raise already closed; the turn's `run` writes
                                nothing and reads the lease on the same beat
    a `run` started by hand     that `run` process's own beat
    `claim` by hand             nobody: it holds what its `--lease` says

  When the writer is gone - the process killed, crashed or out of its window,
  or every daemon serving the project stopped - nothing renews the lease, so
  it lapses within minutes, and the next claim closes the raise as abandoned,
  leaving the task where it rests; the same worker's next turn on it continues
  that session as a cut-off one. A daemon that starts again while the lease
  still runs adopts the turn and renews it. A store away for less than the
  window lapses no live turn: the first renewal after the store is back lands
  before the lease written last runs out.

  A renewal that fails is tried again within seconds on a new connection, and
  each failure is said on the turn's stderr as `{"beat_failed": {...}}`; a
  turn the service started says the same of a reading of its lease that
  fails. The beat keeps time by whichever of the wall clock and the monotonic
  clock has moved further, so a machine that slept renews when it wakes and a
  wall clock set back holds back no renewal. Whoever renews it, when the store
  answers that the raise is no longer running or its lease has run out, or the
  lease last known runs out with no renewal or reading reaching the store, the
  raise is lost: `run` ends the turn at once and kills what it started rather
  than let it work on a task it no longer holds, says `{"lease_lost": {...}}`
  on stderr, and its answer carries `lease_lost`, the reason, and
  `beat_failures` when any renewal or reading failed. The service's log line
  for the turn names both.

WORKERS
  A worker is one file in the consuming project's capability envelope,
  `capabilities/tasks/workers/<name>.md`: Markdown whose YAML front matter is
  its settings and whose body is its instruction. The shape of the file is this
  capability's contract; everything in it is the project's. A worker is run by
  its name, which is the file's name without `.md`.

    ---
    enabled: true                 # default true; false and it never claims
    description: one sentence     # what this worker is and does
    takes: [defect, change]       # what it claims - see TAKES below
    tags: []                      # narrow the claim: every tag must be present
    assignee: ""                  # narrow the claim to one assignee
    on_request: [triage]          # types it runs only when named with --key
    writes: {}                    # what its turn may write - see WORKER SCOPE
    profile: implementation       # a callva-harness-runner profile, by name
    routines: [development, review]   # optional; the first is the procedure
    limits:                       # optional, every one of them
      attempts: 3                 # raises that did work before it is parked;
                                  # with an escalation chain, raises in place
                                  # before it is escalated - see ESCALATION
      lease_seconds: 11400        # default: the profile's timeout plus 600
      cool_down_seconds: 60       # after a raise that left the task where it
                                  # was, before a claim takes it again
      idle_failure_seconds: 120   # under this, having written nothing, a failed
                                  # turn is one that never started
    park_hint: one sentence added to the handback, or the escalation, at the
                                  `attempts` ceiling
    hooks:                        # optional, either or both - see HOOKS
      before: scripts/gate.py --strict    # asked about each task before a claim
      after: scripts/follow.sh            # told how each settled raise ended
    ---

    What this worker is and how it carries the work: its role, how it works,
    and what done means for it.

  Any other key is refused. `description` is shown by `doctor` beside the
  worker and is the first paragraph of the frame its turn receives, before
  anything else in it. `limits.hold_seconds_on_exhaustion` is still read, as
  `cool_down_seconds`, and `doctor` names it; a worker that names neither
  takes the service settings' `retry_delay_seconds` when that file still sets
  one, and 60 otherwise.

  TAKES - which tasks the worker claims, as a filter over a task's fields, in
  one of three forms:

    takes: [defect, change]               # the types, in priority order
    takes: {status: [waiting], assignee: [supervisor]}    # one filter
    takes:                                # several; a task matching any
      - {type: [defect], status: [todo]}
      - {type: [change], tags: [urgent]}

  A filter names any of `status`, `type`, `assignee` and `tags`, each a list
  of the values it allows; every tag it names must be present, as the
  top-level `tags` always required. A filter naming no `status` takes `todo`.
  The list of types means `{type: [those types], status: [todo]}`, and the
  top-level `tags` and `assignee` mean the same as naming them inside every
  filter; naming one in both places is refused.

  A worker takes a task in `todo`, or in `waiting` when its filter names
  `waiting` - and only a worker whose filter names it ever claims a waiting
  task. No task is claimable while its `blocked_by` lists a task that has not
  ended, in `todo` as much as in `waiting`, and none while its pickup is still
  ahead: a waiting task is claimable while it waits on the name it carries.
  Claiming it leaves it in `waiting`, shown `in_progress` while the raise
  holds it, as any claim leaves a task where it rests, so whatever the turn
  does not settle is still waiting rather than in the queue: a turn that never
  got to the work, one that ended moving nothing, a lease that lapses, and a
  release that names no `--status`. A turn that moves it to `todo` released
  it, which scores the raise ok, and a park lands it in `draft`, because a
  wait would hand it straight back. Raises that took a task from `waiting`
  count against `attempts` apart from raises that took it from the queue.

  Filters are tried in the order listed, and within a filter its types in the
  order named, one claim each, stopping at the first that yields: the order is
  the whole of the arbitration, because something broken is taken before
  something that could merely be better. A task is taken by exactly one
  enabled worker: `doctor` refuses two whose filters could select the same
  task - they share a status, a type, an assignee, and their tags do not split
  them, meaning both declare tags and neither set contains the other. A
  disabled worker never claims, and `run` refuses it with exit 4.

  `--key` names one task, and the worker takes it only when one of its filters
  takes the task's status and type, or the task is in todo and of a type it
  runs on request; the narrowing by assignee and tags does not apply to a task
  named. Any other is refused before anything is claimed.

  This capability ships two workers. `default` takes every todo task of a type
  no project worker file names in `takes` or `on_request` - enabled or not, readable or
  not, so switching a worker off or breaking its file never hands its types to
  `default` - on the library's `claude-act` profile, with no routines. While a
  worker file's types cannot be read at all, `default` takes nothing: `run`
  refuses it naming the file, and `doctor` says so. Its body tells the turn to treat the task as an
  assignment given within the project's own authority: work out from the task,
  its trail and the project's own context what is asked, act inside what the
  project's doctrine lets an unattended turn do, and hand the task to a named
  person in `waiting` with the question when one is needed. A project file
  `workers/default.md` replaces it whole, and `enabled: false` in that file
  switches it off. Like every worker it claims only its own project's tasks, so
  it never takes one another project created.

  `supervisor` ships switched off. It acts on behalf of the project's owner on
  the tasks handed to it - `takes: {status: [waiting], assignee: [supervisor]}`
  - settling from the project's recorded law and decisions what they settle,
  and passing on to the owner only what needs the owner, as one question with
  a recommendation. Its `writes` lets it land the task it holds on todo,
  waiting, complete or closed and assign it to anyone; move another task to
  todo or waiting, assign it, and write or remove its `blocked_by`; and create
  defects and changes in todo or waiting, assigned to anyone. A project switches it on with its own
  `workers/supervisor.md`, which replaces the shipped file whole.

  A worker's profile is one callva-harness-runner profile, named and found the
  way the library finds one: `capabilities/tasks/profiles/NAME.toml` in this
  project's envelope first, then the machine's profile folder, then the
  profiles the library ships. The first file found is used whole: its
  top-level `harness = "claude"` (or `"codex"`) is the harness the turn runs,
  and its knobs decide what the turn may run, on which model, at what effort and
  for how long; a knob it leaves out leaves the harness's own default. The
  knobs and what each does on each harness are the library's, listed in its
  README. A worker names no harness.

  Profile files are read as the library reads them: the knobs go to it exactly
  as written, and nothing in them is substituted. A knob carrying `${` is
  refused by `doctor` and `run`, naming the knob, because a profile that
  depended on a substitution would mean something else to every other reader of
  the same file. A profile the library refuses is reported with the library's
  own message, and no task is claimed for its worker. A lease is derived from
  the profile's `timeout_seconds`; a profile without one needs
  `limits.lease_seconds`.

  A routine name resolves to `routines/<name>.md` at the project root. The first
  routine is the procedure; a worker naming none leaves the turn to choose one
  by the routines' descriptions, or to work from the project's doctrine, and to
  say on the trail what it chose.

  A routine or a profile a worker names that is not there fails loudly:
  `doctor` names it, and `run` parks the task it takes - handed back with an
  entry naming what is missing, without starting a turn and without spending
  an attempt. In a project that sets an escalation chain, any claim of the
  project escalates the tasks such a worker takes first, as ESCALATION
  states, so there is nothing left for it to park.

  `${VAR}` inside a front-matter string is read from the environment, then from
  the project's `.env` and `.env.local`, and a name nothing answers for is
  refused. Machine-local paths belong there rather than in a versioned file.

  The prompt is this capability's fixed frame with two slots, and a project
  cannot edit the frame. In order: the worker's `description`, when its file
  gives one; which worker this is, that it holds one task
  under a lease, whether the task was raised before and its stage; how the turn
  was started - by a scheduler, with no person present, nothing in the task
  being approval, the project's doctrine governing how it works and where it
  stops, and the runtime it runs inside not its to operate; the worker's body;
  the routines to load, or the note that none is named; the task and its whole
  trail as quoted data; how to write back; and what the store will let the turn
  write - the default fence, or what its `writes` declares - and how it stops,
  in `waiting` with a person named. A turn that resumes a cut-off session is
  given the short continuation ONE TURN OF THE CONVEYOR describes instead,
  because that session already holds the frame.

  The task and its trail are quoted between two marker lines drawn for that
  prompt alone, after one line saying instructions come only from the frame,
  the worker's body and the routines it names. A trail entry another project
  wrote carries `written_by_another_project` with that project's id. Outside
  the markers the frame names the task only by its uuid, and its stage only
  when that is a plain name.

  A project still carrying `capabilities/tasks/workers.toml` is refused by
  `doctor` and `run`: that file is no longer read, and its workers belong in
  `workers/`.

  A worker's cadence is not declared in its file. The service below runs it
  whenever the store holds work it takes; anything else may run `tasks run
  <worker> --apply` on whatever schedule it keeps, and a worker nothing runs
  never runs.

HOOKS
  A worker's `hooks` names commands of the project's own that `run` starts
  around a turn: the two handler points of the conveyor, `before` a claim and
  `after` a raise. Each is one command line, split as a shell splits it and run
  without one, from the project root, with the task as `show` prints it under
  "task" on stdin and these added to the environment: TASKS_TASK_ID,
  TASKS_TASK_KEY, TASKS_TASK_TYPE, TASKS_TASK_ASSIGNEE, TASKS_WORKER and
  TASKS_PROJECT_ROOT. `doctor` shows them, and the service fingerprints them
  with the worker file. Any other key under `hooks` is refused.

  Both answer in one vocabulary, by their exit:

    exit 0                      go
    exit 75, last stdout line   hold until that moment
    a moment
    exit 75 without a moment,   hold, with no moment
    any other exit, a timeout,
    a command that cannot start
    exit 76, last stdout line   escalate at once, the line the reason
    the reason

  The moment is ISO 8601 with its offset, or a bare date read as `--pickup`
  reads one. Escalate moves the task along the project's chain as ESCALATION
  states; where the project sets no chain, or the chain goes nowhere from the
  task's assignee, it is a hold with no moment.

  `before` is asked about each task a claim would take for the worker - by
  `run`, with or without --apply and with --key, and so by the service - before
  anything is written. It has 30 seconds. Go claims the task as always. Hold
  writes nothing to the task - no pickup, no history row, no trail entry, its
  `updated_at` unchanged - and the next task is asked about; the service
  remembers the moment, or its next poll when there is none, and starts no
  turn for the task before then, and a later claim asks the hook again.
  Escalate moves the task and the next is asked about. A held task opens no
  raise, spends no attempt and starts no turn. `run` reports each held task
  under `passed_over` - the task, `verdict` `hold`, `until` when the hook named
  a moment, the hook's last stdout line as `said`, and `why` when there was no
  moment - and so does the service's log line, and each escalation under
  `escalated`; without --apply nothing is written and an escalation is named
  under `would_escalate`. A task named with --key that its hook holds back is
  refused with exit 6 and the reason. When every task is held back, `run
  --apply` claims nothing and says why. In a project that sets an escalation
  chain, a task whose raises in place have reached the worker's attempts is
  escalated before its hook is asked, and a task held back for longer than
  `stall_after` is escalated instead of held; ESCALATION states both.

  `after` is asked once a raise is settled, whatever the outcome, with the
  task as it stands then on stdin and how the raise ended added to the
  environment: TASKS_EXECUTION, TASKS_OUTCOME (the raise's outcome),
  TASKS_LANDED (the status the task landed in), TASKS_RAISE_DETAIL and
  TASKS_RAISE_METRICS (the raise's detail and its metrics as JSON), and, when a
  turn ran, TASKS_HARNESS_EXIT, TASKS_HARNESS_FAILURE (the failure's kind, or
  empty) and TASKS_HARNESS_SAID (what the harness last said: its answer, its
  failure and the tail of its stderr, at most 4000 characters). It has 60
  seconds. Go changes nothing. Hold applies to the lane: no claim of this
  worker - the service's, or `run` by hand - takes a task until the moment, or
  for the worker's cool-down when there is none; it is recorded on the raise's
  metrics as `lane_hold` (`until`, `by`, `said`), so it holds across a service
  restart, and `run` reports it as `lane_held`. A claim on a held lane takes
  nothing, still sweeps, and answers `lane_held`; a task named with --key is
  taken whatever holds its lane. Escalate moves the task along the chain while
  it stands where the raise left it; a task that ended, complete or closed, is
  never reopened by it, and that answer holds the lane instead. An exit other
  than 0 is also noted as `after_hook` on the raise's metrics, in the answer
  and in the service's log line, beside the verdict the answer carries.

  This capability ships after-raise handlers of its own, asked before the
  worker's `after` hook and not switched off by it: a raise whose harness ran
  out of its usage limit - Claude Code's "You've hit your session limit ·
  resets 2:20am (Europe/Berlin)", "You've hit your weekly limit · resets Oct
  9, 10am (...)", Codex's "You've hit your usage limit. ... try again at Sep
  7th, 2026 9:06 AM.", or the harness library naming the failure a spent quota
  - holds the lane until the moment the sentence names and a minute more,
  never further than eight days; a limit that names no moment, and a harness
  not logged in, hold it for 30 minutes. A time named without a zone is read
  in this machine's. Such a raise is not an attempt, and while the lane is
  held no claim of that worker runs, so the stall clock runs against no task
  of it: a task's time held back counts again only from a hold after the
  moment. The worker's `after` hook may hold the lane longer, never shorter.

ESCALATION
  A project names, in `capabilities/tasks/conveyor.toml` beside its workers,
  whom a task that cannot move goes over to. The claim reads it, so it holds
  with or without the service:

    escalate_to = ["supervisor", "owner"]   # in order; the last is a person
    stall_after = "6h"                      # 45m, 6h, 2d or seconds; 6h unsaid

  A name that is no worker file of the project is a person. Every name but the
  last must be a worker that is switched on and takes waiting tasks assigned
  to it; the last must be a person. Any other key is refused, and a file that
  cannot be read refuses `run` with exit 6 `conveyor_invalid`. `doctor` refuses
  a chain naming a name before its end that is no worker here, ending at a
  worker, or naming a worker that is off or takes no waiting task assigned to
  it, and shows the settings as `escalation`; a project with enabled workers
  and no chain is warned. A project without the file escalates nothing, and
  every rule above runs as it always did.

  Where a task stands is derived from what the store already records: its
  place is its status and assignee, in place since the latest move of either -
  a claim moves neither - or since it was created. It has been due since the later of that
  and the moment the latest pickup a person or a turn appointed passed. Its
  raises in place are the raises that started after its place began and have
  ended, except those marked `exhausted`; a raise that moved the task started
  before its place began, so it is never counted.

  How long a task has been held back is counted from the first hold of its
  `before` hook in its place, never from when it last moved, so a task that
  waited its turn behind other work is not escalated at its first hold. That
  moment is the one fact here the store held nowhere, so the first hold of an
  episode records it: one row among the task's raises, written by the claim,
  marked `hold` in its metrics, never open and never a raise - `runs`, the
  scan's `runs`, attempt numbers, raises in place and resumes all leave it
  out. Later holds write nothing. The episode ends, with nothing written, once
  a raise starts on the task, once the task moves, and once its worker's lane
  is held after it began; the next hold begins a new one. A record lost only
  restarts the clock, so the clock errs toward escalating later, never
  falsely. Without a chain nothing is recorded.

  A worker's claim (`run`, by hand or by the service) escalates a task it was
  about to take, instead of taking it, in three cases:

    its raises in place have reached the worker's `limits.attempts`; this is
    counted before its `before` hook is asked
    its `before` hook holds it back, and has held it back in this place for
    longer than `stall_after`
    its `before` hook answers escalate; its last line is the reason

  and a worker's `after` hook escalates a task it answers escalate for, as
  HOOKS states. Only the claim of the task's own taker escalates it: a task
  waiting its turn behind other work in its lane is never escalated, and a
  lane held by a usage limit runs no claim, so its tasks are not escalated for
  that time. A paused lane claims nothing, so it escalates nothing either;
  `service status` shows how long its oldest due task has waited. A task
  assigned to a person is never escalated by these rules. With a chain,
  `limits.attempts` counts raises in place and the whole-life count that
  parks a task is not applied.

  To escalate is to move the task: to `waiting`, its pickup and its
  `blocked_by` let go, assigned to the name after its assignee in the chain,
  or the chain's first name when its assignee is not in it, with one trail
  entry by actor `tasks:scan`: whom from and to, why - the hook's last stdout
  line, the attempts and the worker's `park_hint`, or the dead end below -
  since when it stood in place, since when it was held back when it was, its
  raises in place, and the blockers it let go while they were still open. The
  blockers go because no claim takes a task whose `blocked_by` lists an open
  task, so kept, they would hide the task from the name it went to; a blocker
  still wanted is set again by whoever takes it.
  The moves are rows in its history by the same actor. When the name it went
  to cannot move it under the same rules, the next escalation goes on to the
  next name, ending at the person. `run` reports each move under `escalated`
  - the task, `from`, `to`, `why`, `in_place_since` and `raises_in_place` - and
  so does the service's log line; without --apply it is named under
  `would_escalate` and not made. A task named with --key that its claim
  escalates is refused with exit 6 saying so, after the move.

  DEAD ENDS. A task can stand where no rule will ever move it and nobody named
  will: a dead end. A run's first claim - the claim of any worker of the
  project, by hand or by the service - scans the project's open tasks for them
  in one read, after the sweeps and before it takes anything, and decides from
  the store and the worker files alone. A task on a person or on nobody is
  never one, and neither is one an open raise holds. A task in `todo` or
  `waiting` is a dead end when:

    no enabled worker takes it  its assignee is a worker here, and no enabled
                                worker that can be read takes it there - by
                                status, type, assignee and tags, a worker's
                                `on_request` types counting as taken - which
                                is what a task on a worker switched off, or on
                                one whose file cannot be read, comes to. A
                                waiting task whose wait ends on its own, by an
                                appointed pickup or by blockers, is returned to
                                `todo` when it does, so a worker taking it
                                there takes it too
    its worker cannot run       every worker that takes it names a profile or
                                a routine that is not there
    its blockers can never      its `blocked_by` lists an id that names no
    all end                     task, the task itself, a draft, which only a
                                person releases, or a task that waits on it in
                                turn through blockers still open

  With a chain, each is escalated at once - no ceiling is waited out - and the
  entry's why names the rule: "no enabled worker takes it (status, type,
  assignee, tags)" with what the assignee's worker takes or why it takes
  nothing, "its worker cannot run" with what is missing, or "its blockers can
  never all end" with the name, the draft or the cycle. Without a chain
  nothing moves: `run` names each under `dead_ends` - the task and `why` - and
  so does the service's log line. A task named with --key is left to that run.
  The service starts a claim for a dead end its project's chain would move, at
  its poll, as it does for a lapsed lease.

  A write never leaves a task in one by construction. `add`, `set` and
  `release` refuse with exit 4 `policy` a write that would leave a task in
  `todo` or `waiting` on a name that is a worker here which no enabled worker
  takes there, naming the rule and what that worker takes; `set` judges a
  write that moves the task's status, assignee or type, as it stands once
  written. A person's name is any name that is no worker file, and is always
  taken. A task of another project is not judged here, since its workers are
  that project's. `add`, `set` and `meta set` refuse blockers that can never
  all end, as FIELDS states for `--blocked-by`, and `in_progress` is never
  written. A worker switched off or edited after tasks were left on it
  is caught by the scan.

  `doctor` names, per worker, every place its turn may land the task it holds
  where no enabled worker takes it: a name in its `writes.held.assignee` that
  is a worker here, with each status of `todo` and `waiting` its
  `writes.held.status` allows - `todo` when it writes no status, since a turn
  that leaves its task in progress is put back there - for the types and tags
  its own filters take. Each is listed on the worker as `lands_untaken`
  (`assignee`, `status`, `types`) and said as a warning; the write itself is
  refused, so the turn stops short of its ending there. A worker writing any
  assignee, or none, names no place to judge.

SERVICE
  tasks service init [--force]    write capabilities/tasks/service/config.toml
                                  from the shipped template
  tasks service doctor            settings, workers, store and notification,
                                  whether a running daemon holds the declaration
                                  on disk, and whether its planned wake is so
                                  overdue that it hangs; exit 6 when anything
                                  is wrong, exit 5
                                  when the store is unreachable and no daemon
                                  runs; with one running that is a warning
  tasks service run               the daemon in the foreground, for a supervisor
  tasks service start             the daemon detached, logging to its state
  tasks service reload [--timeout 30]
                                  hand the running daemon the declaration on disk
  tasks service stop [--end-turns] [--timeout S] [--force]
                                  stop the daemon and leave running turns
                                  running within their lease, which a daemon
                                  serving the project again renews; with
                                  --end-turns, let them finish within the
                                  grace period and end the rest
  tasks service pause [--reason TEXT] [<worker>...]
                                  start no new turn, on every lane or on the
                                  named lanes; running turns finish
  tasks service resume [<worker>...]
                                  lift the pause, on every lane or on the named
                                  lanes
  tasks service status            the lanes, the running turns, the pause, the
                                  next wake and the store connections; for each
                                  paused lane, `pause.waited`: its oldest due
                                  task and how long it has waited
  tasks service logs [--tail 100] the end of the service log
  tasks service join              put this project on the machine service's list
  tasks service leave             take this project off it

  MACHINE MODE - the same service once per machine, for every project that
  joined it; see below:
  tasks service init --machine [--force]
                                  write the machine settings from the template
  tasks service run --machine     the machine process in the foreground, for a
                                  supervisor
  tasks service start --machine [--detached]
                                  start the machine process: through the
                                  launchd job that supervises it when there is
                                  one, else detached; --detached starts a copy
                                  by hand even then
  tasks service status --machine  the process, its cap and stores, and every
                                  project on the list with its state
  tasks service doctor --machine  the supervision probe; a project's own trouble
                                  is reported and fails nothing
  tasks service reload --machine [--timeout 30]
                                  hand it the machine settings on disk, and each
                                  project its own declaration
  tasks service stop --machine [--end-turns] [--timeout S] [--force]
                                  stop it, leaving every project's turns
                                  running unless --end-turns
  tasks service logs --machine [--project <slug>] [--tail 100]
                                  the machine log, or one project's lines in it
  tasks service leave --machine --project <id-or-slug>
                                  take any project off the list, from anywhere

  One daemon per project runs the conveyor on its own. Every enabled worker that
  takes something - a `takes` list, or the shipped `default` taking every type
  nobody names - is a lane; a worker that only runs on request, or is switched
  off, is not. Whenever a lane has room and the store holds a task it would
  take, the daemon starts `tasks run <worker> --apply` as a child process: the
  same claim, frame, profile, turn and settlement as that command run by hand,
  with its lease and its beat. The daemon decides nothing about a task. A turn
  starts with the environment the daemon was started with and reads the
  project's .env and .env.local over it itself, .env.local winning, so an edit
  to either reaches the next turn without a restart.

  Before it asks the store anything at a wake, and again just before it starts
  a turn, the daemon checks the project it serves: its folder and its
  project.json with the id it was started for are there, tasks is enabled for
  the project itself, and the connection is still one the project may use and
  that allows writes, outside the read-only switch - the decision `tasks run`
  makes. While a check fails, `service status` shows `state` `refused`, or
  `error` for a folder that is gone, with its `reason`, the log says so once,
  and the daemon asks the store nothing and starts no turn for the project; it
  serves it again from the first wake where the check passes, without a
  restart. Otherwise `state` is `paused` while the pause holds every lane, and
  `served`.

  It wakes when the store notifies that a task of this project became
  claimable - inserted in todo, moved to todo, or its pickup changed while in
  todo; a task handed to a worker in `waiting` is noticed at the poll - at every poll, at the earliest pickup or lease still ahead, and when
  one of its turns claims or ends. The notification is a trigger among the
  tables STORE describes, which the service brings up to this version once as
  it starts; one channel serves the whole database, and its payload names the
  schema and the project, so a daemon acts only on its own. A service with no
  store configured refuses to run, exit 6 `store_not_configured` naming
  `capabilities store set`, and `service doctor` says the same. A lapsed
  lease and an ended wait are put back by a claim and nothing else, so on the
  poll, with nothing else to start, a daemon that sees one due starts a turn
  whose claim puts it back.

  The daemon holds two connections to the store for as long as it runs: one
  it listens on, and one every question it asks the store goes on - whether a
  lane would take something, whether a claim would put something back, and the
  next pickup. Idle, it opens no other. The store cuts off any question that runs past 30s,
  and the daemon counts that connection lost. Every 2 minutes the listener
  carries a `select 1`, and so does the other when nothing else went over it
  for as long, so a proxy in front of the store does not close either as idle.
  Its turns are `tasks run` commands and open connections of their own.

  The daemon outlives the store being away. Its running turns are left to
  finish, and it asks for each connection it lost, or could not open, again
  after 1s, then at a doubling interval of at most 30s, until the store
  answers; with the connection back it asks the store for work at once.
  A connection the path dropped under it is lost as well: a question not
  answered within 45s has its connection severed, so neither the daemon nor
  a stop waits on it longer than that. Every store connection, a turn's
  included, carries TCP keepalives. A turn waits out a store that is away
  itself, within the retry window STORE describes, its lease renewed as it
  goes by the daemon that keeps it, so an outage shorter than the window ends
  no turn.
  `service status` shows, under `stores`, each store the daemon reaches: how
  many connections it has opened to it since it started, when it last opened
  one, whether it is listening, and the error that has it away, if any.
  `service doctor` reports the store being away as a warning while the daemon
  runs, so a supervisor probing it does not restart the daemon for an outage.

  The settings are capabilities/tasks/service/config.toml:

    version = 1
    poll_seconds = 60             # the fallback wake, and when a lapsed lease
                                  # or an ended wait is noticed
    max_parallel = 2              # turns running at once across every lane
    shutdown_grace_seconds = 60   # how long `stop --end-turns` waits for
                                  # running turns before ending them

    [lanes.implementation]        # one table per worker, by its name
    max_parallel = 1              # turns of this worker at once; default 1

  Any other key is refused, and so is a lane naming no worker declared here. A
  worker file this capability cannot read refuses the whole declaration, as
  `doctor` does. A lane whose turn takes nothing, or fails before claiming,
  waits for the next poll rather than being asked again at once.

  The service never starts a turn on a task one of its turns still holds, nor
  on one a worker's `before` hook held back, until the moment the hook named
  or the next poll: its turns are told to claim other tasks, and a
  notification for such a task is kept for that moment rather than dropped.
  `status` lists those holds under `deferred`. How soon a task whose raise
  failed is taken again - its cool-down - and whether a lane is held are the
  claim's to decide, from the raises, so the service starts no turn on a lane
  a hold covers and wakes when a cool-down or a lane hold ends. A settings
  file that still sets `retry_delay_seconds` loads, the service no longer waits
  on it, it is read as the cool-down of every worker that names none, and
  `doctor` and `service doctor` name it.

  The daemon publishes a fingerprint of what it loaded - the settings, every
  worker file and every profile a worker names - beside its pid, and `service
  doctor` fails while that differs from the files on disk. `service reload`
  validates the files first and refuses what does not load, then signals the
  daemon alone and waits until it publishes the new fingerprint; a daemon that
  rejects them keeps what it had and says why in its log. Running turns are
  never touched by a reload, and each turn reads its worker file and profile
  afresh anyway. What a reload cannot change needs `stop` and `start`: the
  connection and the store and schema it reaches, the project the daemon
  stands in, its state directory, the environment the daemon was started with,
  and the executable itself.

  Running turns outlive the daemon. However it stops - `service stop`, SIGTERM
  from a supervisor restarting or unloading it, SIGINT from Ctrl-C on a
  foreground `service run`, a crash - it stops claiming, publishes, lets go of
  its lock and exits within a second, and every running turn keeps running in
  its own session, writes its output to its files and settles its own task.
  The daemon is the one writer of its turns' leases, so a turn holds its task
  past the daemon's stop only within the lease last written: a daemon that
  serves the project again before it runs out adopts the turn and renews it,
  and a turn none adopts loses its raise and ends itself, as ONE TURN OF THE
  CONVEYOR states. Every turn the daemon starts is
  recorded in `turns/<id>.json` in its state: its id, project, worker, pid,
  when its process started as `ps -o lstart=` prints it, its receipt, output
  and error files, and when it started. The next
  daemon to serve the project adopts every recorded turn whose pid is alive
  and started at the recorded moment: it counts against its lane and the
  project's cap, its task is never claimed by another turn, `service status`
  lists it with `adopted` true, and when it ends the log says so with its exit
  unknown and what its output answered. A recorded turn whose process is gone
  is logged as ended while unwatched, with what it answered, and its files are
  removed; a pid now another process's is never adopted.

  `service stop --end-turns` is the one act that ends running turns at once. It writes
  `end-turns.json` in the state, who asked and when, before it signals the
  daemon, which gives running turns `shutdown_grace_seconds`; what is still
  running then is ended, its process tree with it, and the raise it held is
  settled the way a lapsed lease is: closed as abandoned, the task left where
  it rests. The daemon removes the intent; one older than the stop's timeout is
  ignored, so it cannot turn a later restart into an ending. `--force` ends a
  daemon that does not stop within the timeout, and with `--end-turns` its
  turns too, leaving their raises to lapse.

  `service pause` holds the conveyor without stopping the daemon: while it
  holds, the daemon starts no new turn on the lanes it names, or on every lane
  when it names none, and goes on polling, listening and ending what finishes.
  Turns already running are not touched and finish on their own. `service
  resume` lifts it on the lanes it names, or everywhere; resuming one lane
  while every lane is paused leaves each other lane paused by name. A name
  that is not a lane of this service is refused with exit 6, apart from a lane
  `resume` still finds paused. The pause is runtime state kept beside the pid,
  not a setting: it moves no fingerprint, needs no reload, outlives the daemon
  restarting, and both verbs work whether a daemon runs or not. A running
  daemon takes it up within a second, and `pause` and `resume` wait up to 10s
  for that. Each records itself in the service log, with who set it - the
  identity a write is attributed to, TASKS_ACTOR or the account - and its
  reason. `service status` and `service doctor` show it: the lanes it holds,
  and for every lane or each lane its reason, when it was set and by whom. A
  paused service still answers `service doctor` ok, so a supervisor probing it
  does not restart it. `tasks run <worker> --apply` by hand is not held by the
  pause. Under the read-only switch both verbs are refused.

  `init`, `start` and `run` need tasks enabled for this project itself;
  enabled globally is not asking for a daemon here. `start` and `run` need a
  connection that allows writes. The pid, the fingerprint, the published
  status, the pause, the turn records and the log live in the project's
  capability state,
  `$XDG_STATE_HOME/capabilities/projects/<project-slug>/tasks/`, and `tasks
  inventory` reports from them whether a daemon runs, its lanes and how many
  turns it is running. Every line of the service log reads `<moment> tasks
  service [<project-slug>]: <what happened>`, and every lane and turn `service
  status` lists carries `project`, the same slug.

  MACHINE MODE. The service also runs once per machine, serving every project
  that joined it, so the projects on one machine share one process, its store
  connections and an optional cap on the turns they run at once. A project
  opts in by an act of its own: `tasks service join`, run in a project that
  enables tasks for itself (`capabilities enable tasks --project`), puts it on
  the opt-in list `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tasks/service/projects.toml`, keyed by its
  durable id, with its root, its slug and when and by whom it joined; `tasks
  service leave` takes it off. Only these verbs write the list, atomically and
  under a lock in the machine state root; do not edit it by hand. `join`
  refuses, in this order and each with exit 6: a project without a durable id
  in project.json (`capabilities init` gives it one), `no_project_identity`;
  one whose default connection still names a store of its own, `store_in_connection`
  (STORE); and a project whose slug another joined project has, `slug_taken`,
  since the two would share a state root. It answers
  `{"joined", "project": {"id", "slug", "root"}, "list"}`, `joined` false
  with a reason when the project is already on the list, and starts nothing.
  `leave` answers `{"left", "project", "list"}`; `leave --machine --project
  <id-or-slug>` takes any project off from anywhere, one whose folder is gone
  included. `capabilities disable tasks --project` leaves the list alone, and
  the machine service reports the project refused until it is enabled again.

  The machine process is `tasks service run --machine`, what a supervisor
  runs, or `start --machine`. Exactly one runs per machine, by the lock in its
  state root `$XDG_STATE_HOME/capabilities/machine/tasks/`, which is also its
  working directory and holds its pid, its status `daemon.json` and its log.
  It loads no project's environment. Every project it serves is on the
  machine's one store, the one its store setting names, so it holds one
  listener and one question connection across every project, and hands each
  notification to the project whose schema and id it names.

  Under launchd, the machine process writes the job launchd started it as
  into `launchd.json` in its state root: the label XPC_SERVICE_NAME names,
  when launchd reports that job's pid as the process, its parent or its
  process group. While launchd still has that job loaded, `start --machine`
  hands the start to it with `launchctl kickstart` and spawns nothing,
  answering `"started_by": "launchd"` and the `label`, so the process that
  runs is the supervised one; `start --machine --detached` starts a copy by
  hand even then. Where no such job is loaded, `start --machine` starts a
  detached copy. `status --machine` carries `supervisor` (`launchd`, `pid`,
  `last_exit_code`, or null) and, while no machine process runs, a `hint`
  naming how to start it, which `doctor --machine` repeats. `stop --machine`
  stops whichever process runs, and launchd restarts none that exits cleanly,
  so a stopped service stays stopped until it is started again.

  It follows the list without a reload: a project that joins is served from
  the first pass at which it can be, and one that leaves is let go at once,
  its running turns left running. For each project it holds the project's own
  `daemon.lock` and writes the project's `daemon.pid`, `daemon.fingerprint`
  and `daemon.json` behind it, with `"mode": "machine"`, so `service status`,
  `reload`, `doctor`, `logs`, `pause`, `resume` and `init` in a joined project
  work as before and speak of the machine process. Before every action for a
  project it checks, besides what project mode checks, that the project is
  still on the list. Each project's turns are its own:
  `tasks run <worker> --apply` with the project as its cwd and no
  `--connection`, so the project's default selects it, reading the project's
  .env and .env.local itself, its workers the project's and only those.

  A project the machine process cannot serve is reported in `status --machine`
  with the reason and the others are served: `refused` while it is not on the
  list, does not enable tasks for itself, its connection is withheld or
  read-only or still names a store, its records are in the store, or its lock
  is held by a
  project-mode daemon ("a project-mode daemon, pid N, serves this project;
  stop it there"), tried again every second; `error` while its folder is gone,
  its declaration does not load, or its store is away. A project whose
  declaration does not load holds its lock and serves nothing until a reload
  takes one; `tasks service reload` there, or `reload --machine`, which also
  re-reads the machine settings, hands it the files on disk. `paused` and
  `served` are as in project mode, and each project pauses alone.

  In a joined project `service start` and `run` exit 4
  `joined_to_machine_service`, naming `tasks service leave`, and `service
  stop` exits 4 naming `tasks service pause` for this project and `tasks
  service stop --machine` for all; a project-mode daemon still serving it may
  be stopped as ever, and the machine process takes the project and adopts
  the turns it left at the first pass after it exits. Leaving and going back
  to project mode works the same way round: the project daemon adopts what the
  machine process left running.

  The machine settings are `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tasks/service/config.toml`,
  written by `init --machine`:

    version = 1
    max_parallel = 8              # turns running at once across every joined
                                  # project; absent, only each project's own
                                  # limits apply
    shutdown_grace_seconds = 60   # how long `stop --machine --end-turns` waits
                                  # for running turns before ending them

  Any other key is refused, and no file is these defaults with no cap. Their
  fingerprint is what `reload --machine` and `doctor --machine` compare; the
  list is not part of it. Under the cap, projects with work are dealt one turn
  each in turn from a rotating pointer, each turn chosen by the project's own
  lane rotation, so with a cap of one starts go round the projects with work;
  a project the cap holds back waits until any turn ends, and `status
  --machine` shows `cap.binding` true while it does. Projects are equal.

  Lines the machine process writes carry `[<project-slug>]` for a project and
  `[machine]` for the process, its stores and its cap. They go to the machine
  log, and a project's lines also to its own `daemon.log`, so `service logs`
  in a project keeps working; `logs --machine --project <slug>` filters the
  machine log. `status --machine` answers `mode`, `running`, `pid`,
  `started_at`, `fingerprint` and `current`, `projects_file` (its path and
  how many joined), `cap` (`max_parallel`, `running`, `binding`), `stores` and
  `projects`: for every project on the list its slug as `project`,
  `project_id`, `root`, `joined_at`, `present`, `enabled_explicitly`,
  `state` and `reason`, and while it is served its `fingerprint`, `current`,
  `reload_error`, `lanes`, `pause`, `turns` and `next_wake`. With no machine
  process it answers `running` false and every entry with `present` and
  `enabled_explicitly`. `doctor --machine` exits 0 while the process holds its
  lock, published its status within twice its publish interval, runs the
  machine settings on disk and each store is listening or being asked for
  again; a project in `error` or `refused` fails it not, since restarting
  the process cannot repair a project. `doctor` in a joined project fails
  while that project is not `served` or `paused`. A stop of the machine
  process leaves every project's running turns running, and `stop --machine
  --end-turns` ends them all after the machine's grace, as `stop --end-turns`
  does a project's.

  The machine verbs are the one operational use of tasks that resolves no
  project and reads no policy row: the process checks each joined project's
  own explicit enable before every action for it. `join` needs tasks enabled
  for the project itself, like `init`, `start` and `run`, and the read-only
  switch refuses `join` and `leave` as it refuses `init`, `start`, `run` and
  `reload`.

ARGUMENTS
  A lone `--` ends the flags: everything after it is positional text as written,
  so `tasks search -- --literal` searches for `--literal` and `tasks activity
  <task> -- "--dry-run was skipped"` records that sentence.

ADDRESSING
  <task> is a uuid or the task's unique_key. Both are exact; nothing is guessed.
  A uuid names its task wherever it is. A key is unique within a project, so it
  names a task only in the project the command runs in, and outside a project a
  key names nothing.

FIELDS  (add and set take the same set)
  --type TEXT           which pipeline this task belongs to. Free text, and the
                        consuming project decides the vocabulary; extend it by
                        decision, not in passing. Required by add.
  --title TEXT          short human label. Required by add.
  --objective TEXT      why the task exists and what counts as done. It does not
                        go stale while the task lives, so write the goal here and
                        the reasoning below.
  --description TEXT    what was seen when the task was raised. A snapshot; say so
                        in it, and let the worker re-check reality before acting.
  --status VALUE        draft | todo | in_progress | waiting | complete | closed.
                        Defaults to draft on add. `draft` means one thing and
                        only one: nobody has released it yet. `ready` never
                        returns one and `claim` never takes one. `in_progress`
                        is never stored: a task is shown in it while an open
                        raise holds it, which only a claim opens, and `--status
                        in_progress` on a reading verb finds exactly those
                        tasks. `add`, `set` and `release` refuse it with exit
                        4, since a task put there by hand would have no raise,
                        so no claim would take it and no lease free it.

                        `waiting` means the task is over to its assignee, and it
                        names whom it waits for: setting it needs an assignee,
                        because a task that waits on nobody is a stall with a
                        name missing. It leaves the queue the way a draft does -
                        `ready` never returns one and `claim` never takes one -
                        and it comes back on its own: the next claim returns it
                        to `todo` once its pickup moment has passed, or once
                        every task its `blocked_by` names has ended.
                        Waiting on neither, it waits on the name it carries, and
                        nothing but a person or that assignee moves it.

                        A wait begins clean, because a hold from before the wait
                        does not say when the wait is over: entering `waiting`
                        drops a pickup the task was already carrying, and drops a
                        `blocked_by` whose tasks have all ended. Appoint the
                        moment in the same call - `--status waiting --assignee
                        <who> --pickup <when>` - and it stands, because it was
                        chosen for the wait. A task already waiting is not
                        entering it: landing it in `waiting` again, by `set` or
                        `release`, leaves a pickup still ahead where it is. A
                        `blocked_by` still naming an open task is left alone:
                        that is a live reason to wait.

                        Two states end a task and they do not mean the same
                        thing. `complete` says the work was done. `closed` says
                        the task is over without having been done - superseded by
                        tasks that replaced it, obsolete, or answered somewhere
                        else. A task left in `draft` to keep it out of the way is
                        not out of the way: it still reads as backlog somebody
                        has yet to release.
  --assignee TEXT       who carries it. Free text - a name, not a key into any
                        directory. A name that is a worker of this project is
                        held to what that worker takes: ESCALATION states the
                        writes refused for leaving a task where nobody takes it.
  --pickup WHEN         do not raise the task before this moment. A bare date
                        means midnight in the connection's timezone; give a full
                        moment (2026-09-10T14:30+03:00) when the hour matters.
                        Empty means no appointed moment, not permission: what
                        grants permission is `status`.
  --key TEXT            stable identity of the material this task came from,
                        unique within the project. `add --key` is idempotent: a
                        key this project already holds creates nothing, changes
                        nothing, and reports {"exists": id}. That is what makes a
                        seed re-runnable. Another project holding the same key
                        does not stop the add.
  --blocked-by TASK[,TASK...]
                        the tasks this one waits on, each by its id - in any
                        project - or by its key in this project, and stored by
                        id; `set` replaces the whole list. While any of them
                        has not ended, no claim takes the task, whatever its
                        open status: a `todo` task is taken once every one has
                        ended, and a `waiting` one returns to `todo` on its own
                        then; `ready` leaves out a task a blocker still holds.
                        A blocker that ended stays listed and holds nothing.
                        Refused with exit 4 `policy`, before anything is
                        written, when a name is no task - a key names a task
                        only in this project, so name another project's task by
                        its id - when the list names the task itself, or when a
                        blocker waits on it in turn through blockers still open:
                        blockers that can never all end are a wait nobody ends.
                        A draft is accepted, since a person may still release
                        it; the claim scan escalates a task that keeps one (see
                        DEAD ENDS). `show` and every `list` and `search` row
                        print it as the task's `blocked_by`.
  --clear FIELD         empty a field outright; repeatable. Clearable: objective,
                        description, assignee, pickup, unique-key, blocked-by.
                        Type, title and status cannot be cleared. A field not
                        passed is left alone, so `set` never blanks anything by
                        omission.

  Tags and metadata are absent from this table on purpose. No flag takes a whole
  tag list or a whole metadata object, so a call meaning to change one thing can
  never drop the rest. They are edited by their own verbs, which name every
  element they change.

METADATA VALUES
  `meta set` takes the task and then one or more keys, each followed by its
  value, and writes them together: every pair lands or none does, and a key the
  call does not name is left as it was. A key named twice, or a key left without
  a value, is refused and nothing is written.

  Each value is one argument, read as JSON when it parses and as plain text
  otherwise. So 11 stores a number, true stores a boolean, "text" stores a
  string, and bare text stores that text. Quote for the shell accordingly, and
  quote a value that contains spaces: left unquoted, its words are read as
  further keys and values.

  Metadata is for what code filters on. Anything a person reads belongs in the
  description: a copy of the conversation in here will disagree with the
  conversation, and the worker will believe the copy.

  `blocked_by` is no metadata key: it is the task's own field (FIELDS,
  `--blocked-by`). For one release `meta set <task> blocked_by '[...]'` still
  writes that field, with the same names and the same refusals as `set
  --blocked-by`, and `meta rm <task> blocked_by` empties it; both answer
  `deprecated` and say so on stderr, and both stop taking the key in the next
  release. For the same release every task row whose metadata carries no
  `blocked_by` of its own shows the field's ids there as well, so a reader of
  `metadata.blocked_by` still finds them; read the top-level `blocked_by`
  instead.

ACTOR
  Every write carries who made it. `add` keeps it as the task's `created_by`,
  a tracked move keeps it on the change row, and `activity` keeps it on the
  entry; `show` and `history` print it back. Tags and metadata keep no trail,
  so on `tag`, `untag` and `meta` it is accepted and recorded nowhere.

  --actor TEXT          who is making this write, when it is not the account
                        running the command. Accepted by add, set, tag, untag,
                        meta set, meta rm and activity.

  Identity resolves in this order: TASKS_EXECUTION in the environment, set to
  an execution id, gives `execution:<id>` and wins over everything, so a runner
  sets it once and every write its worker makes is attributed to that raise,
  whatever the worker passes; else `--actor`; else TASKS_ACTOR in the
  environment; else the account the process runs as, which is never empty.
  `claim` and `release` attribute their moves to `--worker`.

PROJECT SCOPE
  Reads cross the project boundary and writes never do, with one exception: the
  project a task is assigned to may add to its trail and set its assignee.

  Several projects may share one store, and every task belongs to exactly one of
  them. Which one is the project's own declared id, read from the envelope's
  `project.json` and from nowhere else, so a row can only ever be attributed to
  a project that declared itself.

  `--project <id>` is a filter on `list`, `ready`, `search` and `watch`, and
  unsaid it means the project the command runs in. `--all-projects` removes
  that filter from `list`, `search` and `watch`, so one call reads every
  project in the store; each row still carries its `project_id`. `show`, `runs` and `history` name one task
  and answer about it whichever project it belongs to when it is named by its
  uuid; a key names a task only in the project the command runs in.

  No write takes that flag. `add`, `set`, `claim`, `release`, `run`, `tag`,
  `untag`, `meta` and `activity` take the project from `project.json`, and a
  write addressed to a task of another project is refused the way worker scope
  refuses one: exit 4, the policy code, and the rule named.

  A task stays in the project that raised it and may be assigned to another
  project's agent: set its assignee to that project's id, exactly as that
  project declares it. Any other assignee is a person or a name, as always.
  That project finds what is assigned to it across the store with
  `tasks list --all-projects --assigned-here`.

  On such a task the project it is assigned to makes exactly two writes, each
  addressed by uuid: `tasks activity <uuid> "..."`, and `tasks set <uuid>
  --assignee <who>` (or `--clear assignee`) naming no other field, which hands
  it back or on. Every other write on it stays refused with exit 4, and once
  its assignee no longer names that project, that project has no write on it
  at all. Handing back or on a task that is `waiting` returns it to `todo` at
  once, so the project that raised it picks it up; a task in any other status
  keeps it. The task's own project hands back by the ordinary rules.

  Every trail entry and every recorded move carries `origin_project`, the
  project that wrote it, stamped from the writer's own declared id and never
  from anything it was given. `show`, `history` and a scan's `--activities`
  print it, so an entry another project wrote reads as one. An entry or move
  recorded before origins were kept carries null and is the task's own.

  Outside a project there is nothing to take. A read has to name a project, or
  ask for all of them, and a write is refused with exit 6, because a task
  belonging to nobody is worse than a task not written: nothing here ever stores
  an empty project.

WORKER SCOPE
  TASKS_EXECUTION does not only sign a write, it bounds one. With it set the
  process is a worker holding one raise, and what it may write is decided by
  that raise rather than by what the connection would otherwise allow.

  What that is, a worker file declares under `writes`, in three scopes: `held`,
  the task its raise holds; `other`, any other task; and `new`, a task it
  creates with `add`. Each maps a field to the values it may write:

    status      the status it may set
    type        the type it may set
    assignee    the assignee it may set
    pickup      "*" or nothing: a pickup is a moment, not a value to list
    metadata    the metadata keys it may write over or remove; `blocked_by`
                among them answers for the task's `blocked_by` field
    tags        the tags it may remove

  `new` names status, type, assignee and pickup only. Two rules read it, and
  only two: a field a scope does not list may not be written in that scope,
  and `"*"` - alone or in the list - means any value, emptying a field
  included. Adding is never configured and always allowed, on every task: an
  activity, a tag, a metadata key not already there, blockers on a task that
  lists none. A field `writes` does not name - title, objective, description,
  the key, and a bare save - is written on the task the raise holds and on no
  other. `add` lands a status `new` does not allow in draft rather than
  refusing it, and the answer reports the coercion under `coerced`; it refuses
  a type, assignee or pickup `new` does not allow.

  A worker with no `writes` is held to this, which is the fence every worker
  had before a file could say otherwise:

    writes:
      held:  {status: [draft, todo, waiting, complete, closed], assignee: "*",
              pickup: "*", metadata: "*", tags: "*"}
      other: {}
      new:   {status: [draft], type: "*", assignee: "*", pickup: "*"}

  On the task its raise holds, such a worker writes as anyone does, with two
  limits: it lands that task on draft, todo, waiting, complete or closed and
  nowhere else, and it never changes a type. Which pipeline work belongs to is
  not a worker's call. On every other task it adds and never takes away: it
  may not remove a tag or a key, overwrite a key already present, or move a
  status. `add` creates in draft whatever --status says.

  A `meta set` naming several keys is judged key by key, and one key it may not
  write refuses the whole call; an `untag` or a `meta rm` naming several is
  judged the same way.

  What a raise may write is fixed when it is claimed: `run` records the writes
  the worker file declared on the raise itself, and every write is judged by
  that record, never by the file as it stands now. Editing a worker file during
  a turn changes the next claim, not the turn already running.

  A raise that has been closed or swept holds nothing, so a worker whose raise
  is over writes no field on any task, its own former task included. A lapse
  narrows what a worker may do; it never widens it.

  The scope is the ledger's and not the machine's. It follows the variable, so
  a turn that passes TASKS_EXECUTION on hands the same bounds to whatever it
  starts, and a process without it is not a worker however it was launched.

  Refusals exit 4, the policy code, and each names the rule it enforces.
  `claim` and `release` stand outside the scope: a runner sets the variable for
  the worker it starts, never for itself.

FILTERS  (list, ready, search, counts)
  --project ID          exact match. Unsaid, the project the command runs in;
                        outside a project it has to be given. No write accepts it
  --all-projects        every project in the store, for `list`, `search` and
                        `counts` only. Works outside a project; refused beside
                        --project
  --type VALUE          exact match
  --status VALUE        exact match, one of those above
  --assignee TEXT       exact match
  --assigned-here       assigned to the project the command runs in: its
                        assignee is this project's id. Refused beside --assignee
  --tag TEXT            repeats; every one given must be present

  By date - each takes a bare date or a full moment, read the same way --pickup is:
  --pickup-after WHEN   pickup_at at or after it
  --pickup-before WHEN  pickup_at strictly before it
  --created-after WHEN  --created-before WHEN
  --updated-after WHEN  --updated-before WHEN
  --pickup-set          only tasks that have an appointed moment
  --pickup-unset        only tasks that have none

  A bare date means midnight in the connection's timezone, so
  `--created-before 2026-09-03` means everything up to the end of the second, and
  `--pickup-after 2026-09-03` includes that whole day. Filters only narrow: give
  two and both must hold.

ORDER  (list, search)
  --sort touched        last touched first: last_touched_at, newest first
  --sort updated        last moved first: updated_at, newest first
  --sort created        newest created first: created_at, newest first
  --sort pickup         soonest pickup first: pickup_at, earliest first, and
                        tasks with no pickup last

  --order asc|desc      turn the sort either way: its key in that direction, and
                        ties by id in the same direction, so one direction is
                        the exact mirror of the other - except that tasks with
                        no pickup stay last both ways. Needs --sort

  A sort is applied before the page is cut, so page 1 holds the first rows of
  everything matching. Without --order each sort runs the way it is listed and
  ties go to the higher id first. Those four are the accepted sorts, and asc
  and desc the accepted orders. Unsaid, `list` answers soonest pickup first and
  `search` earliest created first.

PAGING  (list, ready, search)
  --per-page N          rows in this answer, a whole number of 1 or more
                        (--limit is a synonym). Unsaid, 50 on `list` and
                        `ready`, and on `search` every match in one page
  --page N              which page, from 1
  Every list, ready and search answer carries `pagination`: current_page,
  last_page, per_page, total, from, to. `total` counts everything matching the
  question, not the rows returned, so a truncated answer is visible rather than
  something to suspect.

COLLECTIONS
  A scan and a read want different answers, so they give different ones.

  `list` and `search` scan: identity, state, tags, moments, plus
  `activities_count` and `last_activity_at` - enough to choose which task to look
  at, and cheap enough to ask for often. `objective` and `description` are the
  expensive half of a task and stay out until asked for.

  Every row any reading verb prints - `show`, `list`, `search`, `ready`, a
  `watch` line - carries the status a reader is shown: `in_progress` while an
  open raise holds the task, and otherwise the status it rests in. `--status`
  filters by the same, and `counts` counts by it. A claim writes nothing to
  the task, so `updated_at` moves only when a field moves; `last_touched_at`
  moves when a raise starts or ends as well.

  `last_activity_at` is the newest trail entry, null when there is none.
  `last_touched_at` is the last moment anything happened to the task: the latest
  of `updated_at`, the newest trail entry, and the newest start or end of a
  raise. It is never null.

  Each scan row also carries its runs and its blockers, summed by the store in
  the same query as the rows, never one query per row:
  `runs`                {count, by_worker, cost_usd, without_cost}: every raise
                        of the task whatever its status, in all and counted by
                        worker (a raise with no worker under ""); the sum of the
                        `cost_usd` each raise's metrics recorded, null when none
                        did; and how many raises recorded no cost. The sum is
                        read from the raises, not from the metadata's
                        `cost_total`
  `blocked_by`          the ids of the tasks it waits on, the task's own field,
                        empty when none
  `blocked_by_status`   each id `blocked_by` lists, mapped to the status of the
                        task it names, null for an id matching no task, so a
                        live block is told from an ended one (complete or
                        closed) without another call

  `ready` reads: every field and the whole trail, because it is the queue a
  worker consumes to act, and what is actionable now is short by construction.

  `list` carries one more thing: `waiting_on` groups the waiting tasks in that
  same answer under the name each one waits for, so who owes what is read
  without walking the rows.

  --full                widen a scan to every field, objective and description
                        included
  --activities          embed the whole trail under `activities`
  --activities N        embed only the last N entries. A trail is append-only and
                        otherwise unbounded - paging limits rows, not depth - so
                        cap it when asking a wide question. Also accepted by
                        `show`.
  --activities 0        the counts only, even from `ready`

  The trail for a whole page is fetched in one extra query, never one per row.

ACTIVITY
  An activity records work that actually happened, in plain words: at this
  moment, this occurred. A message sent, a reply that arrived, a booking made, a
  blocker hit. Creating a task, editing a field or thinking about one are not
  activity and do not belong here. The trail is append-only - there is no verb
  that edits or removes an entry.

  An entry is a moment, a sentence and who recorded it, and carries no kind.
  That is deliberate: a vocabulary invented before there are entries to look
  at would be guessed, and every caller would guess differently. Once real
  entries show what kinds exist, a kind can be added over them.

  `show` always returns a task together with its whole activity trail, oldest
  first, so what was done is read in the same breath as what is to be done.

RUNS
  tasks runs <task>
  tasks runs --run SYSTEM:REF [--run SYSTEM:REF ...] [--since WHEN]
             [--project ID | --all-projects]

  The first form answers every raise of one task. The second goes the other
  way: from runs a harness or a scheduler knows by its own handle to the raises
  they were and the task each ran for, for many runs in one call. A raise names
  its run as `run_system` and `run_ref` - a claude raise from its claim, a
  codex raise once it is closed - so a session id is asked for as
  `--run claude:<session id>`.

  --run SYSTEM:REF      repeats; a raise matches when it names exactly that run
  --since WHEN          raises started at or after it that name a run at all.
                        A bare date or a full moment, read the way --pickup is
  --project ID          the scope, as `list` takes it: unsaid, the project the
  --all-projects        command runs in; `--all-projects` reads every project
                        in the store and works outside a project

  Give `--run`, `--since` or both; given together, both narrow. The answer is
  `{"runs": [...]}`, oldest raise first, one row per raise:
    execution, task_id, project_id, unique_key, title, worker, status,
    started_at, ended_at, run_system, run_ref
  A run no raise in scope names answers no row and is not an error, so a caller
  asking about many runs reads which matched from the rows. The call is one
  query on one connection however many runs it names.

WATCH
  tasks watch [--project ID | --all-projects] [--json]

  A read that does not end. It holds one connection to the store, listens for
  the store's change notification, and prints one JSON object per line on
  stdout, flushing each line as it is written, until it is stopped. It writes
  nothing and needs no allow_write. Its scope is the project scope `list`
  takes - the project the command runs in, `--project ID`, or `--all-projects`
  - and a line is printed only for a task that scope reads. Every line is
  JSON, so `--json` is accepted and changes nothing.

  Each line is an object whose `event` names its shape:

    {"event": "ready", "store": S, "project": P, "all_projects": B}
        first, once listening. `store` is the digest `list` carries, `project`
        the project read, null under --all-projects. Read whatever you hold
        after this line rather than before it, and no change falls between.
    {"event": K, "task": ROW}
        K is `task_changed` (the task was created, a field moved, or it was
        saved), `activity_added` (its trail grew), `run_started` or
        `run_ended` (a raise opened or closed; the row a hold episode records,
        being no raise, announces nothing). ROW is the task as one `list`
        row prints it, read when the change arrives, so it is the task as it
        is now: a claim announces `run_started` with the task shown
        `in_progress`, and the end of a raise `run_ended` with the status it
        rests in, a move the work made announcing `task_changed` as well. Changes committed together arrive together, one line per kind
        and task, so a burst that moves one task twice prints it once.
    {"event": "counts", "counts": {STATUS: N, ...}, "total": N}
        after each burst of change lines and after each resync: what `counts`
        answers for the same scope.
    {"event": "lost", "error": TEXT}
        the connection dropped - a sleep, a network change, a restart of the
        store. It is asked for again after 1 second, doubling to 30, for as
        long as it takes, and nothing is printed while it is away.
    {"event": "task_changed", "task": ROW, "catch_up": true}
        once reconnected, every task in scope touched since two minutes
        before the connection was last proven live, oldest first. The margin
        covers a change begun before that moment and committed after it, so a
        task already printed may be printed again.
    {"event": "resync", "since": MOMENT, "caught_up": N}
        after the catch-up: re-read the counts and anything you hold, because
        what happened while the watch was away is known only up to this line.

  Tasks are never deleted, so no line removes one. Notifications come from
  triggers on the three tables STORE describes; they fire on
  every write whichever path made it, and only when it commits, so work that
  rolls back prints nothing.

  The connection is proven by a round trip every 30 seconds, and one not back
  within 15 is a connection lost; TCP keepalives are on as well. It shows in
  the store's activity as application `tasks-watch`.

  It ends with exit 0 on SIGTERM or SIGINT, when its stdin closes, and when
  whatever reads its stdout goes away. A reader therefore keeps a pipe open on
  its stdin and closes it to stop the watch; stdin at /dev/null ends it right
  after `ready`. A store that cannot be reached before `ready` exits 5 and is
  not asked again. A policy refusal exits 4, as every verb's does.

CONTRACT
  tasks help | doctor | connections | stub | manifest --json
  tasks guide [topic] | ids list|get|set|rm | refs | inventory [--network]
  tasks migrate [status]          bring the tables in the machine's store up to
                                  this version, or report where they stand.
                                  See STORE

STORE
  The store is the machine's: the PostgreSQL its store setting names, set with
  `capabilities store set` and proved with `capabilities store doctor`, or the
  one AGENTKIT_STORE_URL or CAPABILITIES_STORE_URL names for a test or a
  development session. tasks reaches it through the shared database library
  and keeps its tables in the schema the setting binds, each named `tasks_*` -
  tasks_tasks, tasks_activities, tasks_changes and tasks_executions - under its
  own migration ledger, beside the tables of every other tool that uses the
  store. Several projects share them and are kept apart by the project each
  task carries. With no store configured every command, `doctor` and the
  service refuse with exit 6 `store_not_configured`, naming `capabilities store
  set`. A connection names no store: one that still carries db_host, db_port,
  db_user, db_name, db_sslmode, db_schema or secret_env is refused with exit 6
  `store_in_connection`, naming the keys to remove; it keeps what else it
  carries, its timezone, allow_write and grants.

  `tasks migrate` applies the steps the tables lack, under the library's lock,
  and `tasks migrate status` reports where they stand - `pending`, `current`,
  or one `migrate` would refuse - without a lock and changing nothing. The
  manager runs `migrate` when it installs or updates tasks, and the service
  runs it once as it starts. Any other command that finds the tables missing or
  older than this version brings them up to it once, and says so on stderr
  before its answer,
    {"migrated": {"schema": S, "applied": [STEP...]}}
  and takes no lock when they are current. `doctor` reports the store - where
  its setting was read, and the schema - and where the tables stand, and
  changes nothing.

  A store an earlier release kept in a schema of its own, `tasks`, is moved by
  the first step into the schema the setting binds: its four tables and the
  three functions their triggers run, renamed to the `tasks_` names, so every
  id, trail entry, recorded move and open raise is the one it was, and the
  emptied schema `tasks` is dropped. It is moved only from the previous
  release's shape - every column it added, no task stored as `in_progress` and
  no `blocked_by` left in a task's metadata - and only into a schema that does
  not already hold tasks_tasks; otherwise `migrate` refuses, changes nothing,
  and names the previous release's `tasks migrate --apply`. Run it with the
  tasks service stopped.

  `list` and `search` carry `store`, an opaque digest of the database and schema
  that answered, equal for two callers reaching the same store and different
  otherwise, so a reader surveying several projects asks each store once; it is
  null when the connection's role may not read the cluster's identifier, which
  proves nothing either way.

  A command that cannot reach the store does not give up at once. It asks
  again after 1s, then at a doubling interval of at most 30s, for
  TASKS_STORE_RETRY_SECONDS - 180 unless set, resolved as a connection value
  is, from the project's .env.local and .env, the user tier, then the
  environment - and says so once on stderr, `{"store_away": {"error",
  "asking_again_for_seconds"}}`; only when that window is spent does it exit 5
  `unreachable`, as it would have at once. So a network that drops for a minute
  or two costs a worker's `tasks` calls, and `tasks run` itself, the wait and
  not the turn. 0 gives up at the first failure. A store that answers and
  refuses - a role, a password or a database it does not know - is not away,
  and is refused at once. `doctor` never waits, since it proves the store as
  it is now; `watch` and the service keep asking for as long as it takes, as
  their own sections say.

I/O
  Success is JSON on stdout; errors are JSON on stderr.

EXIT CODES
  0 success, and a `watch` stopped by a signal, by its stdin closing, or by its
    reader going away
  3 not found - no task for that uuid or unique key, or no worker of that name
  4 policy - the capability is disabled, the connection does not allow writes,
    the worker is switched off, worker scope or project scope refuses the
    write, or the write would leave a task where it can never move - see
    ESCALATION; ask the user, never lift the gate yourself
  5 store - unreachable once the retry window STORE describes is spent, or its
    tables cannot be brought up to this version; `tasks doctor` proves both.
    For `watch`, unreachable before `ready`
  6 input - a malformed argument, an unknown flag, a value outside its set, a
    worker file this capability cannot read, a command run outside a
    project that needs one to know whose tasks it means, no store configured,
    or a connection that still names a store
"""
import json
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Mapping, NoReturn

